persistent-scratch NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

This is the news of e8-persistent-scratch, the E8 port of
persistent-scratch.  'C-h P' shows it under the package's description.


* Changes in persistent-scratch 0.1.1

** It needs E8 0.4.0 or newer, rather than 0.3.1.


* Changes in persistent-scratch 0.1.0

This is the first release, a port of persistent-scratch 0.3.9.  It
needs E8 0.3.1 or newer.

** '*scratch*' is kept across sessions, saved to a file and read back.
Its text, point and mark, major mode and narrowing are kept.
'persistent-scratch-setup-default' restores it and turns on
'persistent-scratch-autosave-mode'.

** 'persistent-scratch-save' saves the scratch buffers to a file.
'persistent-scratch-restore' reads them back, replacing the text of a
buffer of the same name.  'persistent-scratch-save-file' says where.

** The scratch buffers can be saved to a file you name and read back.
With 'persistent-scratch-save-to-file' and
'persistent-scratch-restore-from-file'.

** 'persistent-scratch-autosave-mode' saves every few minutes.
It saves when E8 quits too, and turning it on restores the scratch
buffers first, once a session.
'persistent-scratch-autosave-interval' is the seconds between saves,
or ["idle", N] to save after N idle seconds.

** 'persistent-scratch-mode' makes 'C-x C-s' save the scratch buffers.
'C-x C-w' does too, rather than asking for a file.

** 'persistent-scratch-what-to-save' chooses what is kept.
Besides each buffer's name and text: "major-mode", "point" with the
mark, and "narrowing".

** A function says which buffers are scratch buffers.
It is 'persistent-scratch-scratch-buffer-p-function'; by default only
'*scratch*' is.

** Dated backups of each save can be kept.
Set 'persistent-scratch-backup-directory'.
'persistent-scratch-backup-filter' says which to delete, as
'persistent-scratch-keep-n-newest-backups' and
'persistent-scratch-keep-backups-not-older-than' make, and
'persistent-scratch-new-backup' starts a new one at the next save.

** The save file is in persistent-scratch.el's format.
E8 and Emacs can read each other's, so one file can be shared.
